                    <p class=MsoNormal align=center style="text-align:center;"><b><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-ascii-font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-hansi-font-family:'Times New Roman';
mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-weight:bold;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="Times New Roman">Course </font></span></b><b><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-weight:bold;
font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;">Description</span></b><b><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-ascii-font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-hansi-font-family:'Times New Roman';
mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-weight:bold;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-spacerun:'yes';">&nbsp;</span></span></b><b><span
                                    style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-weight:bold;
font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;">(</span></b><b><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-ascii-font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-hansi-font-family:'Times New Roman';
mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-weight:bold;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="Times New Roman">in English</font></span></b><b><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-weight:bold;
font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;">)</span></b><b><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-weight:bold;
font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></b></p>

                </td>
                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><h3>Course Description</h3>
<p>Chemical elements constitute the fundamental building blocks of the material world. An in-depth understanding of elemental chemistry is essential to research across a broad range of fields, including the normal functioning of living organisms, the mechanisms underlying disease, and the discovery and development of therapeutics. Different chemical elements possess distinct physicochemical properties and can form diverse molecular species, thereby performing different biological functions. The detection and analysis of these elements and their associated molecules provide important tools for elucidating their mechanisms of action and establishing the fundamental knowledge required for disease diagnosis and treatment as well as drug development.</p>
<p>This course is organized around <strong>chemical elements</strong>, with <strong>molecular properties</strong> as its scientific foundation and <strong>clinical applications of elements</strong> as its primary focus. It introduces the roles and applications of elemental chemistry in medicine and healthcare from multiple perspectives, ranging from fundamental elements in the environment to clinically relevant metals and transition metals, as well as emerging technologies and recent advances in biomedical and pharmaceutical research.</p>
<p>The course consists of the following three modules:</p>
<p><strong>1. Nonmetal Elements</strong></p>
<p>This module covers the properties of oxygen as an element, the physicochemical properties of molecular oxygen, and its biological functions. Topics include the biological roles of reactive oxygen species (ROS), their applications in drug development, methods for ROS detection, and photodynamic therapy. Fluorescent dyes and super-resolution fluorescence imaging will also be introduced. The biological effects of small gaseous molecules, including O₂, CO₂, NO, and CO, will be discussed together with recent research advances in this field. The module also covers the properties and biological functions of fluorine and iodine, their applications in pharmaceutical development, and their roles in radionuclide imaging.</p>
<p><strong>2. Metal Elements</strong></p>
<p>This module introduces the physicochemical properties and biological functions of sodium and potassium, with particular emphasis on their roles in neuronal signal transmission and their relevance to drug development for neurological disorders. The physicochemical properties and biological functions of calcium will also be discussed, including its role in neuronal signaling and the development and application of genetically encoded fluorescent indicators and other protein-based fluorescent probes.</p>
<p><strong>3. Transition and Other Medically Relevant Metal Elements</strong></p>
<p>This module examines medically relevant metal elements, including gallium, lutetium, mercury, iron, and zinc. Topics include their characteristic chemical properties and biological functions, conventional elemental analysis, fluorescence-based detection, and their emerging applications in areas such as gene editing and in vitro diagnostics.</p>
<p>In addition to elemental chemistry, the course provides students with foundational knowledge of several interdisciplinary areas closely related to modern biomedical research. These include <strong>molecular imaging</strong> (such as fluorescence imaging, molecular probes, and radionuclide imaging), <strong>drug development</strong> (including cell-based therapeutics), and <strong>emerging biomedical technologies</strong> (such as gene editing).</p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="height:19.9000pt;">
